{
 "section_summary": "This section continues a Spanish-language, strongly pro-Japanese account of East Asian affairs, concluding a discussion of Japan’s postwar diplomacy and praising Japanese economic development in Manchuria. It then surveys the Chinese Revolution and Kuomintang rule, attacks foreign concessions and Chinese anti-Japanese boycotts from a hostile perspective, and begins a legal defense of Japan’s treaty rights after the Mukden conflict. The extract ends mid-argument over the 1915 treaty, Manchurian railway and residency rights, and the competence of the League of Nations.",
