   "about": "A polemical defense of Japanese policy and treaty rights that contrasts Japan’s development of Manchuria with the instability and nationalism of republican China.",
   "summary": "The excerpt finishes an account of Japan’s diplomatic reorientation after the First World War and presents Japanese policy as peaceful, commercially driven and restrained. It praises the South Manchurian Railway and Japanese investment for transforming Manchuria, using extensive statistics on trade, industry, agriculture and public services. Subsequent chapters recount the Chinese Revolution, Sun Yat-sen, Yuan Shikai and Kuomintang rule while depicting China as politically fragmented and hostile to foreigners, with particular emphasis on anti-Japanese boycotts and intimidation. The final pages argue that the underlying Sino-Japanese dispute concerns China’s alleged violations of binding treaties, especially the 1915 agreements governing Liaodong, the railways, residence, landholding and mining in southern Manchuria and eastern Inner Mongolia.",
